# Madara Uchiha - Wake Up to Reality Speech

**Original Japanese Speech** with **Romaji**, **Literal Breakdown**, **Natural Translation**, and **Analysis**

---

### 1. Opening Command
**Japanese:**  
現実を見ろ。

**Romaji:**  
Genjitsu o miro.

**Literal Breakdown:**
- 現実 (Genjitsu) = reality
- を (o) = object marker
- 見ろ (miro) = look / see (imperative)

**Natural Translation:**  
"Wake up to reality." / "Face reality."

**Analysis:**  
A powerful, direct command. Madara forces Obito (and the audience) to stop living in illusion and confront the harsh truth of the world.

---

### 2. The Nature of the World
**Japanese:**  
この世は思い通りにはいかぬことばかりだ。

**Romaji:**  
Kono yo wa omoidōri ni wa ikanu koto bakari da.

**Literal Breakdown:**

| Japanese     | Romaji          | Meaning                              |
|--------------|-----------------|--------------------------------------|
| この世      | Kono yo        | This world                           |
| は          | wa             | (topic marker)                       |
| 思い通り    | omoidōri       | as one wishes / as planned           |
| に          | ni             | in the manner of                     |
| は          | wa             | (emphasis)                           |
| いかぬ      | ikanu          | does not go (literary negative)      |
| こと        | koto           | things                               |
| ばかり      | bakari         | only / nothing but                   |
| だ          | da             | is                                   |

**Natural Translation:**  
"In this world, nothing ever goes as planned."

**Analysis:**  
This line establishes the core philosophy of the speech — life is uncontrollable and full of disappointment. The use of old-fashioned "ikanu" gives it a dramatic, philosophical tone.

---

### 3. The Longer You Live
**Japanese:**  
長く生きれば生きるほど、現実は苦しみと痛みと虚しさだけが漂っていることに気づく……

**Romaji:**  
Nagaku ikereba ikeru hodo, genjitsu wa kurushimi to itami to munashisa dake ga tadayotte iru koto ni kizuku……

**Natural Translation:**  
"The longer you live, the more you realize that reality is filled with nothing but suffering, pain, and emptiness."

**Analysis:**  
Madara describes growing older as a process of painful awakening. The imagery of "drifting" suggests these negative emotions are constant and inescapable, like fog.

---

### 4. Core Philosophy

**Japanese:**  
いいか……  
この世のすべてにおいて、光が当たるところには必ず影がある。  
勝者という概念がある以上、敗者は同じくして存在する。  
平和を保ちたいとする利己的な意志が戦争を起こし、  
愛を守るために憎しみが生まれる。

**Romaji:**  
Ī ka……  
Kono yo no subete ni oite, hikari ga ataru tokoro ni wa kanarazu kage ga aru.  
Shōsha to iu gainen ga aru ijō, haisha wa onajiku shite sonzai suru.  
Heiwa o tamochitai to suru riko-tekina ishi ga sensō o okoshi,  
Ai o mamoru tame ni nikushimi ga umareru.

**Natural Translation:**  
"Listen… In everything in this world, wherever there is light, there is always shadow. As long as the concept of victors exists, losers will also exist. The selfish desire to maintain peace causes war, and hatred is born to protect love."

**Analysis:**  
Madara explains the inescapable duality of existence. Every positive (light, peace, love, victory) automatically creates its negative counterpart. This is the foundation of his nihilistic worldview.

---

### 5. The Chain of Fate
**Japanese:**  
これらは因果の連鎖であり、切り離すことはできぬ。  
この世界の因果を断ち切りたい……

**Romaji:**  
Korera wa inga no rensa de ari, kirihanasu koto wa dekinu.  
Kono sekai no inga o tachikiritai……

**Natural Translation:**  
"These are a chain of cause and effect, and they cannot be separated. I want to sever the cause and effect of this world…"

**Analysis:**  
Madara views the world as an unbreakable loop of karma. He rejects gradual change and instead wants to completely break the system.

---

### 6. Madara’s Ultimate Goal
**Japanese:**  
勝者だけの世界、平和だけの世界、愛だけの世界。それらだけの世界を創る。

**Romaji:**  
Shōsha dake no sekai, heiwa dake no sekai, ai dake no sekai. Sorera dake no sekai o tsukuru.

**Literal Breakdown:**

- 勝者だけ (Shōsha dake) = only victors  
- 平和だけ (Heiwa dake) = only peace  
- 愛だけ (Ai dake) = only love  
- それらだけ (Sorera dake) = only those things  
- の世界 (no sekai) = a world of  
- を創る (o tsukuru) = I will create

**Natural Translation:**  
"A world of only victors, a world of only peace, a world of only love. I will create a world consisting solely of those."

**Analysis:**  
This is the climax of the speech. Madara wants to create a one-sided utopia using the Infinite Tsukuyomi — a world without suffering, loss, or duality.

---

### 7. Closing
**Japanese:**  
俺は……うちはの亡霊……  
まさにこの現実は……地獄だ。

**Romaji:**  
Ore wa…… Uchiha no bōrei……  
Masa ni kono genjitsu wa…… jigoku da.

**Natural Translation:**  
"I am… the ghost of the Uchiha…  
Truly, this reality… is hell."

**Analysis:**  
Madara presents himself as a vengeful spirit of the Uchiha clan, declaring the current world irredeemable. Ending with "jigoku da" (this is hell) is extremely powerful and bleak.

---

**Full Natural English Version (Combined):**

"Wake up to reality. In this world, nothing ever goes as planned. The longer you live, the more you realize that reality is filled with nothing but suffering, pain, and emptiness.

Listen… Wherever there is light, there will always be shadow. As long as there are winners, there will be losers. The selfish desire to keep peace causes war, and hatred is born to protect love.

These things form an unbreakable chain of cause and effect. I want to sever that chain. 

I will create a world of only victors, a world of only peace, a world of only love. I will create a world consisting solely of those.

I am… the ghost of the Uchiha…  
Truly, this reality… is hell."

